{
  "passage": "Elements can be broadly divided into metals and non-metals. Metals such as iron, copper, aluminium and gold are usually hard and shiny. They are malleable, which means they can be beaten into thin sheets, and ductile, which means they can be drawn into wires. Aluminium foil used to wrap food and copper wires used in electrical fittings are made possible by these properties. Metals are good conductors of heat and electricity, which is why cooking vessels are made of metal and electric wires are made of copper. Metals also make a ringing sound when struck, so school bells are made of metal. Non-metals such as sulphur, carbon, oxygen and phosphorus behave very differently. Solid non-metals are generally soft and dull, and they break into pieces when hammered. They are poor conductors of heat and electricity. There are a few exceptions. Mercury is a metal that is liquid at room temperature. Graphite, a form of carbon, is a non-metal that conducts electricity, and it is used in the electrodes of cells. Diamond, another form of carbon, is the hardest natural substance known. Metals and non-metals also react differently with oxygen. Metals form oxides that are basic in nature, while non-metals form oxides that are acidic in nature. Sodium is so reactive that it is stored in kerosene.",
